Q: Wordpress error log not working A: <p>Your code is correct, but WordPress only writes to debug.log when an actual PHP error occurs.</p>
<p>Make sure the following lines are placed above “That’s all, stop editing” in wp-config.php:</p>
<pre><code>define('WP_DEBUG', true);
define('WP_DEBUG_LOG', true);
define('WP_DEBUG_DISPLAY', false);
@ini_set('display_errors', 0);
</code></pre>
<p>WordPress creates wp-content/debug.log only when an error is triggered. If there is no PHP error, the file will not be created.</p>
<p>To test logging, add this temporarily to any theme file:</p>
<pre><code>error_log('Test error');
</code></pre>
<p>Reload the website and then check wp-content/debug.log.</p>
<p>On some hosting providers, PHP logging is disabled. In that case enable it using php.ini or .htaccess:</p>
<pre><code>php_flag log_errors on
php_value error_log wp-content/debug.log
</code></pre>
<p>Your configuration is correct — the reason nothing appears is that no error is being generated or the server is blocking logging.</p>

Q: Automatic CreatedAt and UpdatedAt with onModelCreating and a generic BaseEntity A: <p>Arguably by far the easiest option would be to move the non-generic parts of the <code>BaseEntity&lt;TKey&gt;</code> into a non-generic class/interface. For example:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public abstract class AuditedEntity
{
    public DateTime CreatedAt { get; set; }
    public DateTime UpdatedAt { get; set; }
}

public abstract class BaseEntity&lt;TKey&gt; : AuditedEntity
{
    public TKey Id { get; set; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Which should also allow you to use the generic <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.entityframeworkcore.changetracking.changetracker.entries?view=efcore-10.0#microsoft-entityframeworkcore-changetracking-changetracker-entries-1" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>ChangeTracker.Entries&lt;TEntity&gt;()</code></a> method:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var entries = ChangeTracker
   .Entries&lt;AuditedEntity&gt;()
   .Where(...)
</code></pre>

Q: How to enqueue CSS in a WordPress child theme without overriding parent styles? A: <p>In a WordPress child theme, you should enqueue the <strong>parent theme CSS first</strong>, then the <strong>child theme CSS</strong>, and use a dependency so the order is maintained.</p>
<p>Add this code to your <strong>child theme’s <code>functions.php</code></strong> file:</p>
<pre><code>function child_theme_styles() {
    // Parent theme stylesheet
    wp_enqueue_style(
        'parent-style',
        get_template_directory_uri() . '/style.css'
    );

    // Child theme stylesheet (depends on parent)
    wp_enqueue_style(
        'child-style',
        get_stylesheet_uri(),
        array('parent-style')
    );
}
add_action('wp_enqueue_scripts', 'child_theme_styles');
</code></pre>
<p>Explanation:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>get_template_directory_uri()</code> → loads <strong>parent theme</strong> CSS</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>get_stylesheet_uri()</code> → loads <strong>child theme</strong> CSS</p>
</li>
<li><p>Dependency array (<code>array('parent-style')</code>) ensures the <strong>parent loads first</strong></p>
</li>
<li><p>This allows you to <strong>override only specific styles</strong> in the child theme without breaking parent styles</p>
</li>
</ul>
